00:37:04,741 --> 00:37:09,221
{\an8}This is Oak Park neighborhood, so this is
known as one of the nice areas of Chicago.

705
00:37:10,581 --> 00:37:12,501
- A very beautiful area.
- [Mo] Yeah.

706
00:37:12,581 --> 00:37:15,741
[Monique] We passed by a whole bunch
of shops, but it's cute bakeries,

707
00:37:15,821 --> 00:37:18,901
it's ethnic food places,
like the taco place over there.

708
00:37:20,061 --> 00:37:24,621
Everything is more "bougie" out here
and so things are, like, more expensive.

709
00:37:24,701 --> 00:37:28,061
But we're on our way
to West Garfield Park,

710
00:37:28,141 --> 00:37:29,981
which is where I grew up.

711
00:37:30,061 --> 00:37:32,301
You'll definitely be able
to see the change.

712
00:37:32,381 --> 00:37:33,901
[tense music playing]

713
00:37:38,141 --> 00:37:40,301
[Monique] I call it, like, a food desert.

714
00:37:41,741 --> 00:37:44,941
So, it's fast food everywhere.

715
00:37:45,021 --> 00:37:48,301
I know one neighborhood,
there's a McDonald's, there's a Taco Bell,

716
00:37:48,381 --> 00:37:52,781
there's a Wendy's, there's a Popeyes,
there's Domino's, there's Subway.

717
00:37:52,861 --> 00:37:55,301
It's like, okay,
now where's the grocery store?

718
00:37:55,381 --> 00:37:58,741
You'll see, this is one of
the grocery stores back there.

719
00:37:58,821 --> 00:38:01,941
And, like, they have pictures
of groceries on there,

720
00:38:02,021 --> 00:38:05,661
but when you actually walk in, you just
got chips and juice, things like that.

721
00:38:05,741 --> 00:38:07,781
- There's no carrots there?
- No carrots.

722
00:38:07,861 --> 00:38:09,381
Mushrooms? Tomatoes in there?

723
00:38:09,461 --> 00:38:12,301
No. Maybe eggs,
if you go to, like, a good one.

724
00:38:12,381 --> 00:38:17,541
That's mad because even in ours,
there's eggs in... Eggs are a minimum.

725
00:38:17,621 --> 00:38:21,221
[Monique] The way things are,
you have to take what's in front of you.

726
00:38:21,981 --> 00:38:25,141
[professor] The concept of food desert
comes from the UK.

727
00:38:25,221 --> 00:38:27,141
Actually from Scotland.

728
00:38:27,221 --> 00:38:31,901
{\an8}It is used to characterize areas
where there's only unhealthy food.

729
00:38:32,501 --> 00:38:36,421
And fast food companies definitely target
lower-income communities.

730
00:38:37,261 --> 00:38:39,661
This is part of their bread and butter.

731
00:38:39,741 --> 00:38:44,861
And it is estimated that in 2025,
approximately 19 million people,

732
00:38:44,941 --> 00:38:48,381
or 6% of the U.S. population,
live in a food desert.

733
00:38:48,461 --> 00:38:50,501
And indeed it is somewhat predatory,

734
00:38:50,581 --> 00:38:54,301
because these are consumers
who may not have other options.

735
00:38:55,141 --> 00:38:57,461
- We're here. Do you wanna try Sharks?
- [Mo] Let's try Sharks.

736
00:38:58,461 --> 00:39:01,741
[dispatcher] <i>For your safety,
this parking lot is being recorded.</i>

737
00:39:01,821 --> 00:39:05,341
[Monique]
You can get 20 wings plus fries for $15.

738
00:39:05,421 --> 00:39:08,181
That's a lot of chicken
for very, very cheap.

739
00:39:08,821 --> 00:39:10,981
Also, I've noticed
there's no tables and chairs.

740
00:39:11,061 --> 00:39:13,581
No, this is not the type of place
where you sit down and eat.

741
00:39:13,661 --> 00:39:15,821
- [Mo] Fair enough.
- It's fast food. Get it to go.

742
00:39:15,901 --> 00:39:18,021
- [cashier] Here you go, sir.
- Thank you , my brother.

743
00:39:18,101 --> 00:39:20,061
Appreciate you, man.

744
00:39:20,141 --> 00:39:23,301
Food deserts aren't
just an American problem.

745
00:39:23,381 --> 00:39:24,821
Back home in the UK,

746
00:39:24,901 --> 00:39:28,501
the poorest neighborhoods have
five times more fast food spots

747
00:39:28,581 --> 00:39:29,781
than the wealthier ones,

748
00:39:29,861 --> 00:39:35,501
and there's over 1.2 million people
living in food deserts across the UK.

749
00:39:35,581 --> 00:39:37,661
- Is this Mum?
- Hey! What's going on, you guys?

750
00:39:37,741 --> 00:39:39,461
- How you doing? You okay?
- [mother] I'm well.

751
00:39:39,541 --> 00:39:41,661
- Let me take my shoes off.
- [mother] You don't have to.

752
00:39:41,741 --> 00:39:43,421
[Mo] I have to, Mum. I'm in your house.

753
00:39:44,021 --> 00:39:45,901
- Chicken!
- Yes. So here you go.

754
00:39:45,981 --> 00:39:46,821
Thank you.

755
00:39:46,901 --> 00:39:48,781
[Mo] Quintonele, Mini Mo's mum,

756
00:39:48,861 --> 00:39:53,341
works in healthcare and knows
all about the effects of food deserts.

757
00:39:53,421 --> 00:39:55,661
- [Quintonele] It's very disheartening.
- Mmm.

758
00:39:55,741 --> 00:39:58,701
[Quintonele] The liquor stores,
and the fast food places,

759
00:39:58,781 --> 00:40:00,461
{\an8}and the abandoned buildings,

760
00:40:00,541 --> 00:40:04,181
{\an8}and, um, just everything
that goes with that.

761
00:40:04,261 --> 00:40:06,101
- Like, you can look out that window...
- Yeah.

762
00:40:06,181 --> 00:40:07,821
[Quintonele] ...and you see those buildings.

763
00:40:07,901 --> 00:40:10,541
Someone living down there,
their access to care,

764
00:40:10,621 --> 00:40:14,381
their access to food,
their access to all of those things.

765
00:40:14,461 --> 00:40:19,861
Their life expectancy is
almost 16 years more than mine,

766
00:40:19,941 --> 00:40:21,101
me living here.

767
00:40:21,181 --> 00:40:22,261
Oh, my God!

768
00:40:22,341 --> 00:40:25,021
- That's something called the death gap.
- Yeah.

769
00:40:26,141 --> 00:40:31,621
We know that those chicken places
and the fast food places are not the best,

770
00:40:31,701 --> 00:40:33,741
but it's cheap.

771
00:40:33,821 --> 00:40:37,301
I can feed my kids and I would know
that they would be full.

772
00:40:37,381 --> 00:40:41,581
Do you ever feel like
with some of these food deserts,

773
00:40:41,661 --> 00:40:46,581
that they're designed
to keep people eating bad food?

774
00:40:46,661 --> 00:40:49,221
I mean, yeah, I do. I do.

775
00:40:49,301 --> 00:40:54,181
And it's very manipulative.
It's very misleading. Um...

776
00:40:54,261 --> 00:40:59,381
- And even more, the consequences are dire.
- Yeah.

777
00:40:59,461 --> 00:41:02,541
We're dying. And do you think
that food doesn't play a part in that?

778
00:41:03,141 --> 00:41:06,141
It does play a part,
because that's high blood pressure,

779
00:41:06,221 --> 00:41:10,981
that's high cholesterol,
that's blood sugar, diabetes.

780
00:41:11,061 --> 00:41:13,501
All of these things,
it's being forced into the community

781
00:41:13,581 --> 00:41:16,301
because you are what you eat.

782
00:41:16,381 --> 00:41:19,581
- Yeah.
- And so it's, like, doesn't...

783
00:41:22,981 --> 00:41:24,141
It just...

784
00:41:25,101 --> 00:41:27,061
It's frustrating
and it doesn't make sense.

785
00:41:27,141 --> 00:41:28,341
Do you find it...

786
00:41:29,381 --> 00:41:33,461
- Do you find it hard?
- Yeah. It's hard. It's hard.

787
00:41:33,541 --> 00:41:36,541
And it shouldn't be that hard,

788
00:41:36,621 --> 00:41:39,541
especially for something
as simple as food.

789
00:41:42,181 --> 00:41:43,021
Thank you.

790
00:41:43,101 --> 00:41:45,101
- Yay! You're welcome.
- I appreciate your time.

791
00:41:45,181 --> 00:41:47,381
Thanks for having me, Mum.
Really appreciate that.

792
00:41:47,461 --> 00:41:52,941
I never knew what a food desert was
when I started this journey.

793
00:41:55,141 --> 00:41:57,861
But when you drive into that neighborhood,

794
00:41:58,381 --> 00:41:59,541
it's real, man.

795
00:42:00,661 --> 00:42:02,941
And I don't come
from a privileged background,

796
00:42:03,021 --> 00:42:04,141
know what I mean?

797
00:42:04,221 --> 00:42:07,141
Like, I grew up
in a poor environment, but,

798
00:42:07,941 --> 00:42:13,341
just knowing that the simple things
that we would take for granted,

799
00:42:13,421 --> 00:42:15,741
people don't even have access to...

800
00:42:17,061 --> 00:42:18,501
in <i>America.</i>

801
00:42:20,621 --> 00:42:21,741
[groans]

802
00:42:21,821 --> 00:42:22,781
Man!

803
00:42:24,181 --> 00:42:26,901
We have our problems
in the UK too, you know.

804
00:42:26,981 --> 00:42:31,221
People are going to food banks.
There's a cost of living crisis.

805
00:42:31,821 --> 00:42:34,461
But this isn't just,

806
00:42:34,541 --> 00:42:38,101
"Oops, we didn't put
any vegetables on the corner."

807
00:42:38,981 --> 00:42:40,621
It is by design.

808
00:42:41,341 --> 00:42:44,421
And it's there
to keep the people unhealthy.

809
00:42:45,741 --> 00:42:48,421
Oh, man. This is heavy, man.

810
00:42:51,581 --> 00:42:53,021
Oh, man.

811
00:42:54,821 --> 00:42:56,301
[somber music playing]

812
00:42:56,381 --> 00:42:58,661
[Schmidt] Everywhere you go,
you're surrounded by

813
00:42:58,741 --> 00:43:02,261
these, what they would call,
distribution outlets.

814
00:43:02,341 --> 00:43:06,261
And the idea is to just surround
people with your product

815
00:43:06,341 --> 00:43:07,781
and marketing for your product,

816
00:43:07,861 --> 00:43:11,781
and they compete with one another
to own the food environment.

817
00:43:13,141 --> 00:43:16,221
[marketing expert]
There is a term in marketing called CLV.

818
00:43:17,701 --> 00:43:19,781
"Customer Lifetime Value."

819
00:43:19,861 --> 00:43:25,581
{\an8}And if I can start
and groom a customer from a kid,

820
00:43:25,661 --> 00:43:28,181
{\an8}oh, they're gonna be with me for years.

821
00:43:29,341 --> 00:43:33,221
And when brands are able
to connect with people,

822
00:43:33,301 --> 00:43:36,421
they increase their chances
of getting people to buy,

823
00:43:36,501 --> 00:43:37,941
to eat, to consume.

824
00:43:38,021 --> 00:43:39,821
{\an8}Can I please get a Big--

825
00:43:39,901 --> 00:43:42,581
{\an8}<i>♪ What I need from you is
A Chicken Big Mac ♪</i>

826
00:43:42,661 --> 00:43:44,301
{\an8}Someone stole your Hottie Sauce!

827
00:43:44,381 --> 00:43:47,061
{\an8}[Collins] Black culture,
Black people tend to be

828
00:43:47,141 --> 00:43:50,261
{\an8}the tip of the spear
of all things that tend to be cool,

829
00:43:51,261 --> 00:43:54,061
{\an8}whether it's the music,
the dance, the vernacular...

830
00:43:54,141 --> 00:43:56,381
{\an8}I'm absolutely craving
some nugs right now.

831
00:43:56,461 --> 00:44:01,301
{\an8}[Collins] ...which is why you see brands
partnering with these cultural creators

832
00:44:01,381 --> 00:44:05,541
{\an8}in hope that people go,
"That's cool. I wanna be a part of that."

833
00:44:07,701 --> 00:44:09,861
[Mo] It's only been two-and-a-half weeks,

834
00:44:09,941 --> 00:44:12,261
but I'm now realizing
that I've had a lifetime

835
00:44:12,341 --> 00:44:14,621
of being groomed by fried chicken.

836
00:44:15,701 --> 00:44:17,941
[Mo in commercial]
<i>Yes, I have been to Nando's before.</i>

837
00:44:18,021 --> 00:44:22,661
{\an8}[Mo] Not only that, but reflecting back
on the voiceover work I did for Nando's...

838
00:44:22,741 --> 00:44:25,621
{\an8}Listen, I feel uneasy admitting this, but...

839
00:44:25,701 --> 00:44:27,061
I'm part of the problem.

840
00:44:31,901 --> 00:44:33,901
[introspective music playing]

841
00:44:42,581 --> 00:44:45,181
I've eaten almost 50 meals now,

842
00:44:45,261 --> 00:44:49,581
and I have to be honest, I think
I've bitten off more than I can chew.

843
00:44:50,301 --> 00:44:51,821
I've definitely put some weight on.

844
00:44:51,901 --> 00:44:54,461
When we was in Chicago,
I had to buy bigger t-shirts.

845
00:44:55,901 --> 00:45:00,741
It's very rare that men say, "Oh man,
I just don't feel good in myself."

846
00:45:01,701 --> 00:45:03,461
I was quite naive in thinking,

847
00:45:03,541 --> 00:45:07,181
well, I'm healthy,
how much weight can it really be?

848
00:45:07,261 --> 00:45:09,621
You know, how unhealthy can it really be?

849
00:45:09,701 --> 00:45:12,701
How much is it gonna
mess up my mood that much?

850
00:45:13,781 --> 00:45:14,741
I need help.

851
00:45:14,821 --> 00:45:18,421
And apparently in Michigan University,
there's a whole department

852
00:45:18,501 --> 00:45:21,501
that analyzes how fast food
like fried chicken

853
00:45:21,581 --> 00:45:23,621
is messing with people's minds.

854
00:45:26,021 --> 00:45:27,101
[Gearhardt] Come on in!

855
00:45:28,141 --> 00:45:29,861
- Hello!
- [Gearhardt] Hi, Mo!

856
00:45:29,941 --> 00:45:32,141
- Lovely to meet you.
- Welcome to my lab.

857
00:45:33,181 --> 00:45:34,461
[Mo] Wow!

858
00:45:34,541 --> 00:45:38,101
{\an8}Exactly, and you're gonna actually taste
some of our wares today.

859
00:45:38,181 --> 00:45:39,621
{\an8}This is so weird.

860
00:45:42,021 --> 00:45:44,501
- I'm going to go for a drumstick.
- Yes.

861
00:45:46,541 --> 00:45:49,501
[Gearhardt] I would love to ask you
about the past few weeks

862
00:45:49,581 --> 00:45:51,541
and what your journey's been like.

863
00:45:52,061 --> 00:45:53,421
[Mo] When I'm eating this food,

864
00:45:54,021 --> 00:45:55,741
it feels like you're having a great time,

865
00:45:55,821 --> 00:45:58,581
until the food is finished
and you're by yourself and you realize...

866
00:45:58,661 --> 00:46:02,021
It happened yesterday and you're like,
"I'm so lonely. It feels so lonely here."

867
00:46:02,101 --> 00:46:03,741
[Gearhardt] Yeah. You feel your mood sink.

868
00:46:03,821 --> 00:46:08,381
[Mo] Yeah. And then with the job
that I'm in, doing comedy,

869
00:46:08,461 --> 00:46:12,461
I often find myself looking for
the fun in life, or the funny.

870
00:46:12,541 --> 00:46:13,421
[Gearhardt] Yeah.

871
00:46:14,021 --> 00:46:17,141
[Mo] But even in the short
space of time, it's just like,

872
00:46:18,221 --> 00:46:19,941
I can't be bothered to do that.

873
00:46:20,021 --> 00:46:24,221
- And then, if I'm a little bit moody...
- Mmm.

874
00:46:24,301 --> 00:46:29,181
...then I really wanna treat myself now
to a better experience with food.

875
00:46:29,261 --> 00:46:34,061
So, I will make sure
that if I have some wings,

876
00:46:34,141 --> 00:46:36,621
- I might get a chicken sandwich as well.
- Yeah.

877
00:46:36,701 --> 00:46:40,061
- Do you ever feel kind of sluggish?
- Oh, yeah.

878
00:46:40,141 --> 00:46:42,221
Just like you're slowed down a little bit?

879
00:46:42,301 --> 00:46:45,381
Then the naps as well. And it's
not like a power nap, because I feel--

880
00:46:45,461 --> 00:46:47,261
- Almost like you're drugged.
- Oh my...

881
00:46:47,341 --> 00:46:49,381
I feel like that's how I... Where you just...

882
00:46:49,461 --> 00:46:51,861
[Gearhardt] Not like,
"I need sleep and I wake up restored."

883
00:46:51,941 --> 00:46:53,621
You're like, "I gotta sleep this off."

884
00:46:53,701 --> 00:46:58,461
Oh, my God.
It's like you tracked me somehow,

885
00:46:58,541 --> 00:47:02,221
because that, word for word,
is exactly what happens.

886
00:47:02,301 --> 00:47:03,221
[Gearhardt] Mmm-hmm.

887
00:47:03,301 --> 00:47:05,421
All right,
here's another question for you.

888
00:47:05,501 --> 00:47:09,661
Is there anything
where you kind of wanted to stop

889
00:47:09,741 --> 00:47:11,941
but you felt compelled
to kind of keep going?

890
00:47:12,021 --> 00:47:16,301
Yeah. There's a lot of times
where I've been eating food, and...

891
00:47:17,381 --> 00:47:19,301
- I just have to finish it.
- Mmm-hmm.

892
00:47:19,901 --> 00:47:25,981
Do you ever find yourself looking
to cope or deal with being down and off

893
00:47:26,061 --> 00:47:28,461
by eating more of this sort of food?

894
00:47:28,541 --> 00:47:29,741
[Mo] Yeah.

895
00:47:29,821 --> 00:47:32,741
[Gearhardt] These sorts of foods,
and this is mostly what people are eating...

896
00:47:32,821 --> 00:47:34,341
It does make you moodier.

897
00:47:34,421 --> 00:47:37,901
One of the biggest links
is actually depression and anxiety.

898
00:47:37,981 --> 00:47:40,141
- Does that resonate at all?
- [Mo] Massively.

899
00:47:40,221 --> 00:47:45,061
But all of a sudden, if you take it away,
you're gonna start craving it again.

900
00:47:45,141 --> 00:47:48,501
What you're describing sounds like drugs.
It's an addiction.

901
00:47:48,581 --> 00:47:49,501
Yeah.

902
00:47:49,581 --> 00:47:52,741
Even the anxiousness... and...

903
00:47:52,821 --> 00:47:56,261
Brain fog is something
that I don't often get,

904
00:47:56,341 --> 00:47:58,421
but I'm finding myself forget words.

905
00:47:58,941 --> 00:48:02,661
[Gearhardt] What's really striking
is the questions

906
00:48:02,741 --> 00:48:04,621
I was walking through with you

907
00:48:04,701 --> 00:48:07,301
are the diagnostic criteria

908
00:48:07,381 --> 00:48:10,661
for how we understand whether someone's
starting to show an addiction...

909
00:48:10,741 --> 00:48:13,901
- Oh, wow.
- ...from tobacco, to alcohol, to cocaine.

910
00:48:13,981 --> 00:48:16,461
When you know it's making you feel bad,

911
00:48:16,541 --> 00:48:20,181
and yet there's this part of you
that's still chasing that fix.

912
00:48:21,821 --> 00:48:26,221
The way that these foods
are created to just mainline,

913
00:48:26,301 --> 00:48:29,821
you know, high hits of reward
rapidly into your body

914
00:48:29,901 --> 00:48:32,701
but then crash pretty quickly,

915
00:48:32,781 --> 00:48:36,741
it's how we create
every addictive substance under the sun.

916
00:48:36,821 --> 00:48:42,181
And so it sounds like you are
starting to feel that addictive pull.

917
00:48:42,261 --> 00:48:43,581
Yeah. It's...

918
00:48:44,341 --> 00:48:46,221
I didn't think I would be...

919
00:48:47,421 --> 00:48:49,861
heavily influenced by fried chicken.

920
00:48:49,941 --> 00:48:50,861
[Gearhardt] Yeah.

921
00:48:50,941 --> 00:48:53,221
It's so shocking

922
00:48:54,101 --> 00:48:57,821
that this food is doing that.

923
00:49:00,381 --> 00:49:02,861
[Abood] Human brain is very complex.

924
00:49:03,501 --> 00:49:06,461
Part of the brain
is called the hypothalamus.

925
00:49:06,541 --> 00:49:10,941
It tells us eat, stop eating,
you're hungry, you're not hungry.

926
00:49:11,781 --> 00:49:16,101
We lose that as a direct effect
of those foods

927
00:49:16,181 --> 00:49:20,181
because that pathway
have become imbalanced,

928
00:49:20,261 --> 00:49:23,061
and then food addiction starts,

929
00:49:23,661 --> 00:49:29,701
and it can ruin physical
and mental health in the long run.

930
00:49:30,461 --> 00:49:32,541
[Gearhardt] One of
the most shocking things we've found

931
00:49:32,621 --> 00:49:33,781
is that 12% of children

932
00:49:33,861 --> 00:49:38,341
would meet the criteria for an addiction
with ultra-processed foods.

933
00:49:38,421 --> 00:49:42,981
And as a mother, that really scares me
for the future for our children.

934
00:49:44,381 --> 00:49:45,381
[sighs heavily]

935
00:49:56,061 --> 00:49:59,701
I'm eating this food
and I just, like, don't feel like myself.

936
00:50:04,821 --> 00:50:07,181
Look in the mirror,
and I don't look like myself.

937
00:50:11,141 --> 00:50:12,381
I feel low, man.

938
00:50:12,461 --> 00:50:14,981
I feel so low. I feel so by myself.

939
00:50:15,701 --> 00:50:17,221
[breath hitches]

940
00:50:36,221 --> 00:50:39,781
[sobbing] So much to take in, man.
It's so hard.

941
00:50:42,381 --> 00:50:43,501
Fucking hell.

942
00:50:46,901 --> 00:50:47,981
[breathing raggedly]

943
00:50:50,501 --> 00:50:52,741
Ah, let me log this off, man.
Fucking hell.

944
00:51:00,461 --> 00:51:02,461
[moody, bluesy music playing]

945
00:51:09,141 --> 00:51:10,701
I'm not feeling this today.

946
00:51:11,341 --> 00:51:14,941
Truth be told, I was definitely naive
when I started this journey,

947
00:51:15,021 --> 00:51:17,181
but the more I head down this rabbit hole,

948
00:51:17,261 --> 00:51:20,501
the more I'm questioning
my lifelong love of this food.

949
00:51:21,261 --> 00:51:23,781
[farming advocate]
Fried chicken is everywhere,

950
00:51:23,861 --> 00:51:27,141
and the animals,
they have to come from somewhere.

951
00:51:28,701 --> 00:51:30,341
Oh, that's nasty, man.

952
00:51:30,901 --> 00:51:34,261
{\an8}But what's going on
behind the closed doors

953
00:51:34,341 --> 00:51:39,941
{\an8}is something which the industry itself
likes to keep away from the public eye.

954
00:51:41,261 --> 00:51:42,501
[Mo] I've looked it up,

955
00:51:42,581 --> 00:51:46,421
and in America, almost all of their
chicken comes from factory farms.

956
00:51:47,541 --> 00:51:48,861
And we're catching up.

957
00:51:48,941 --> 00:51:51,141
In the UK, we're at 95%.

958
00:51:52,301 --> 00:51:55,461
[Lymbery] Factory farming,
the mega farming of animals,

959
00:51:55,541 --> 00:52:01,621
started in the US, but it's very much
now deeply ingrained in the UK.

960
00:52:01,701 --> 00:52:04,301
There are now more than
a thousand of them,

961
00:52:04,381 --> 00:52:08,061
hidden away, but nevertheless on the rise.

962
00:52:08,661 --> 00:52:09,621
[Mo] Check this,

963
00:52:09,701 --> 00:52:13,061
{\an8}there's just a handful of companies
that supply the majority of chickens

964
00:52:13,141 --> 00:52:16,501
to supermarkets
and fast food chains in America.

965
00:52:17,621 --> 00:52:19,981
People call them Big Chicken,

966
00:52:20,941 --> 00:52:24,061
and one of the Big Chicken states
is Arkansas.

967
00:52:25,261 --> 00:52:26,661
That's my next stop.

968
00:52:26,741 --> 00:52:29,181
Specifically, a town called Springdale,

969
00:52:29,261 --> 00:52:34,181
home of the biggest American
chicken producers, Tyson Foods.

970
00:52:37,021 --> 00:52:38,981
{\an8}[commercial voiceover] <i>At Tyson Foods,</i>

971
00:52:39,061 --> 00:52:42,301
{\an8}<i>we serve with safety,
accountability, and delight.</i>

972
00:52:43,141 --> 00:52:45,941
{\an8}[Mo] Wow. Seeing Tyson's promo video,

973
00:52:46,021 --> 00:52:49,301
{\an8}for a capitalist corporate giant,
they seem really nice.

974
00:52:49,421 --> 00:52:53,701
{\an8}[commercial] <i>Our leadership shines
through our billion-dollar brands.</i>

975
00:52:53,781 --> 00:52:56,141
{\an8}<i>We feed the world like family.</i>

976
00:52:57,501 --> 00:53:01,701
{\an8}One thing that is true
is that it's a billion-dollar brand.

977
00:53:01,781 --> 00:53:04,181
What Tyson has done
is really set the standard

978
00:53:04,261 --> 00:53:05,941
for the meat-packing industry.

979
00:53:06,661 --> 00:53:09,381
[Mo] And if you wanna win
the Big Chicken game,

980
00:53:09,461 --> 00:53:14,341
all you need to do is play it like Tyson.
Come on. Step right up, Alice.

981
00:53:14,421 --> 00:53:19,261
[Driver] So, Tyson are one of the pioneers
of the vertically-integrated model,

982
00:53:19,341 --> 00:53:23,421
which means that it owns
every single part of the supply chain.

983
00:53:24,581 --> 00:53:26,741
{\an8}From the hatchery, to the chickens,

984
00:53:26,821 --> 00:53:29,261
{\an8}to the slaughter, to production,

985
00:53:29,341 --> 00:53:32,461
{\an8}like chicken nuggets,
and even into trucking.

986
00:53:33,381 --> 00:53:37,701
So I think of it as like a black hole,
like, bringing in every single thing,

987
00:53:37,781 --> 00:53:39,421
the entire universe.

988
00:53:39,501 --> 00:53:43,061
It's a way to control
financially every aspect.

989
00:53:45,541 --> 00:53:47,021
[Mo] Thanks, Alice.

990
00:53:47,101 --> 00:53:49,341
Tyson makes so much money

991
00:53:49,421 --> 00:53:55,141
that almost every Big Chicken company
in the world has replicated their model.

992
00:53:55,221 --> 00:53:58,341
In the UK,
our biggest players are Moy Park,

993
00:53:58,421 --> 00:54:01,381
Hook 2 Sisters and Avara Foods.

994
00:54:01,461 --> 00:54:05,301
They control around 70%
of our chicken market,

995
00:54:05,381 --> 00:54:09,781
processing around
800 million chickens a year.

996
00:54:09,861 --> 00:54:12,781
And by process, I mean kill.

997
00:54:12,861 --> 00:54:13,741
[chicken squawks]

998
00:54:19,861 --> 00:54:21,541
[Mo] I wanted to meet with Tyson,

999
00:54:21,621 --> 00:54:24,421
but when I told them
more about the film, they went quiet.

1000
00:54:24,501 --> 00:54:27,421
So instead, I found a local activist,

1001
00:54:27,501 --> 00:54:30,741
who fights for the rights
of poultry workers.

1002
00:54:30,821 --> 00:54:32,781
She's gonna tell me
what's really going on.

1003
00:54:32,861 --> 00:54:36,461
{\an8}- How are you? Welcome to Arkansas.
- I'm good.

1004
00:54:36,541 --> 00:54:38,661
[Licolli] I'm gonna give you a tour,

1005
00:54:38,741 --> 00:54:42,101
for you to understand
a little bit of the power

1006
00:54:42,181 --> 00:54:45,581
of Tyson in this particular town.

1007
00:54:45,661 --> 00:54:47,781
- Yeah.
- And they build roads,

1008
00:54:47,861 --> 00:54:52,061
and they give money
to churches, to charities.

1009
00:54:52,141 --> 00:54:54,821
Ah, I see this. Tyson sports complex.

1010
00:54:54,901 --> 00:54:58,901
- They're kind of buying their way here.
- Exactly. Exactly.

1011
00:54:59,541 --> 00:55:01,221
[Mo] Oh, look, there's a poultry...

1012
00:55:01,301 --> 00:55:05,301
[Licolli] Yes. But we cannot enter,
of course, 'cause it's private.

1013
00:55:07,341 --> 00:55:11,461
[Driver] I spent the last five years
writing about Tyson Foods,

1014
00:55:11,541 --> 00:55:13,861
and I've never been inside a plant,

1015
00:55:13,941 --> 00:55:17,861
and that is because states like Arkansas
have ag-gag laws.

1016
00:55:18,821 --> 00:55:22,541
It means that you cannot set foot
on the property of Tyson

1017
00:55:22,621 --> 00:55:27,301
with the purpose of documenting
anything related to the facility,

1018
00:55:27,381 --> 00:55:31,341
and the fine in Arkansas
is up to $5,000 per day.

1019
00:55:32,501 --> 00:55:34,821
- Oh, wow, it's so big!
- Yes.

1020
00:55:36,261 --> 00:55:38,381
- [Mo] These are the chickens.
- [Licolli] Yes.

1021
00:55:38,461 --> 00:55:39,301
[Mo] Oh, man.

1022
00:55:39,381 --> 00:55:41,381
Whoo! They smell!

1023
00:55:44,101 --> 00:55:45,461
[Mo] Here come the trucks.

1024
00:55:47,141 --> 00:55:48,661
And they're squashed, man.

1025
00:55:48,741 --> 00:55:52,701
Every day, all day long,
they are killing the birds.

1026
00:55:52,781 --> 00:55:54,861
But then they will be in church...

1027
00:55:54,941 --> 00:55:57,581
[Mo] Oh, my God. There's even feathers.

1028
00:55:57,661 --> 00:55:59,141
- Yeah.
- There's feathers everywhere.

1029
00:55:59,701 --> 00:56:00,621
[Licolli] Yeah.

1030
00:56:01,141 --> 00:56:03,541
So that is spun my head.

1031
00:56:05,301 --> 00:56:08,781
I feel scared for us a little bit,
'cause this is a big corporation.

1032
00:56:08,861 --> 00:56:12,181
- [Licolli] Yeah.
- [Mo] And I know in America, money talks.

1033
00:56:12,981 --> 00:56:14,301
[Licolli] There is the police now.

1034
00:56:14,381 --> 00:56:16,261
[Mo] There's a little police presence.

1035
00:56:16,341 --> 00:56:19,461
In order for these corporations
to get along,

1036
00:56:19,541 --> 00:56:22,861
there has to be a cozy relationship,
right...

1037
00:56:22,941 --> 00:56:24,661
- [Mo] Yeah.
- ...with the politicians

1038
00:56:24,741 --> 00:56:26,821
and police enforcement and everything.

1039
00:56:27,741 --> 00:56:29,261
[Mo] It feels quite scary.

1040
00:56:29,341 --> 00:56:31,541
I know they're watching us.
I know they are.

1041
00:56:31,621 --> 00:56:34,701
- Yeah, it feels...
- They are observing us.

1042
00:56:34,781 --> 00:56:38,741
I know the police have stopped a bit
further up. I don't know if that's for us.

1043
00:56:38,821 --> 00:56:41,621
Shall we cross? Yeah, let's cross.

1044
00:56:41,701 --> 00:56:43,821
We don't wanna aggravate the police.

1045
00:56:44,501 --> 00:56:47,141
I'm Black, you're Hispanic.
We have not got a chance.

1046
00:56:47,221 --> 00:56:48,741
[both laughing]

1047
00:56:51,501 --> 00:56:55,421
[Licolli] When I began doing this work,
and I will ask the workers, like,

1048
00:56:55,501 --> 00:56:56,661
"What do you want?"

1049
00:56:56,741 --> 00:56:58,901
The workers will say,

1050
00:56:58,981 --> 00:57:00,781
"We just wanna feel

1051
00:57:00,861 --> 00:57:04,581
that we are treated better
than the chicken that we kill."

1052
00:57:04,661 --> 00:57:06,061
- You know?
- [Mo] Oh, man.

1053
00:57:06,861 --> 00:57:09,501
[Driver] I think there's things
people can't imagine

1054
00:57:09,581 --> 00:57:12,581
because the people who do work
in these processing plants

1055
00:57:12,661 --> 00:57:14,501
legally can't say very much.

1056
00:57:14,581 --> 00:57:17,941
Is this something that's just happening
here in Arkansas,

1057
00:57:18,021 --> 00:57:22,021
or is this happening all around America?

1058
00:57:22,101 --> 00:57:23,781
Everywhere in America.

1059
00:57:23,861 --> 00:57:27,741
You know, it's the problem
with the whole meat industry.

1060
00:57:29,661 --> 00:57:33,061
{\an8}[Driver] Workers showed me
videos from inside facilities.

1061
00:57:33,541 --> 00:57:37,701
{\an8}There's workers that have to kill
the chickens that aren't actually dead,

1062
00:57:37,781 --> 00:57:41,381
{\an8}but the birds are coming down
the line 175 birds a minute.

1063
00:57:41,461 --> 00:57:43,941
{\an8}People are working elbow to elbow,
with very sharp knives,

1064
00:57:44,021 --> 00:57:45,461
with metal mesh gloves,

1065
00:57:45,541 --> 00:57:47,701
and you're deboning the chicken

1066
00:57:47,781 --> 00:57:49,141
in three to five seconds,

1067
00:57:49,221 --> 00:57:52,061
for eight to ten hours at a time.

1068
00:57:52,141 --> 00:57:53,381
And workers told me that

1069
00:57:53,461 --> 00:57:56,541
"There is no rest because
they're trying to turn me into a machine."

1070
00:57:59,381 --> 00:58:03,061
[Mo] One of the biggest issues
workers in poultry plants face

1071
00:58:03,141 --> 00:58:05,421
is being denied bathroom breaks.

1072
00:58:07,701 --> 00:58:09,221
[Licolli] The lines cannot stop,

1073
00:58:09,301 --> 00:58:12,061
so when workers want
to go to the bathroom,

1074
00:58:12,141 --> 00:58:14,461
and nobody gives them the break,

1075
00:58:14,541 --> 00:58:17,781
workers urinate on the line,

1076
00:58:17,861 --> 00:58:19,701
- peeing on themselves.
- Fucking hell...

1077
00:58:19,781 --> 00:58:25,061
We have heard a lot of cases
of workers being forced to wear diapers,

1078
00:58:25,141 --> 00:58:27,541
and that is just a humiliation.

1079
00:58:27,621 --> 00:58:28,821
- You know?
- Oh, man.

1080
00:58:28,901 --> 00:58:32,741
No workers should be exposed
to such exploitations

1081
00:58:32,821 --> 00:58:34,821
for us to have a chicken sandwich.

1082
00:58:38,901 --> 00:58:41,741
[Mo] She's a courageous woman,
really trying to make a difference,

1083
00:58:41,821 --> 00:58:45,021
but no one has listened to her.

1084
00:58:45,101 --> 00:58:48,061
Fast food ain't coming here
to see how this all works, and...

1085
00:58:48,141 --> 00:58:49,981
I was like, "Oh, my God, this is crazy

1086
00:58:50,061 --> 00:58:52,661
that our chicken
is being produced like this."

1087
00:58:52,741 --> 00:58:55,381
They're like, "We didn't see it,
so it doesn't matter."

1088
00:58:55,461 --> 00:58:57,301
"Let's get some more of them drumsticks."

1089
00:58:57,981 --> 00:59:00,101
In the industry, these workers,

1090
00:59:00,181 --> 00:59:03,621
who are doing one of the most
dangerous jobs in the United States,

1091
00:59:03,701 --> 00:59:09,181
are risking their lives for, you know,
what is slightly above minimum wage,

1092
00:59:09,261 --> 00:59:13,901
in conditions in which they could
suffer from amputation or hospitalization,

1093
00:59:13,981 --> 00:59:16,381
'cause this is very large machinery,

1094
00:59:16,461 --> 00:59:21,261
and someone has to get inside
and clean this machinery.

1095
00:59:22,221 --> 00:59:26,101
A worker at a Tyson plant in Alabama
was decapitated.

1096
00:59:26,901 --> 00:59:29,701
{\an8}Tyson said, you know, "We had nothing
to do with that because

1097
00:59:29,781 --> 00:59:33,421
{\an8}that labor was provided
by a third-party provider."

1098
00:59:34,101 --> 00:59:37,941
{\an8}That's how they distance themselves
from illegal labor practices.

1099
00:59:40,061 --> 00:59:44,101
[Mo] Concerns about labor practices
go beyond the processing plant.

1100
00:59:44,181 --> 00:59:45,461
Questions have been raised

1101
00:59:45,541 --> 00:59:49,021
about conditions
across the Big Chicken industry,

1102
00:59:49,101 --> 00:59:52,021
including on farms.

1103
00:59:52,101 --> 00:59:55,101
In the chicken industry,
you're seeing undocumented workers,

1104
00:59:55,181 --> 00:59:56,701
you're seeing refugees,

1105
00:59:56,781 --> 01:00:01,141
and they're very unlikely
to complain about anything.

1106
01:00:02,941 --> 01:00:05,301
[Mo] To understand
the working environment,

1107
01:00:05,381 --> 01:00:08,781
I've managed to make contact
with an undocumented worker.

1108
01:00:08,861 --> 01:00:10,421
[Mo] Thank you for meeting me.

1109
01:00:10,501 --> 01:00:11,581
[Mo] He's a chicken catcher,

1110
01:00:11,661 --> 01:00:16,701
and has worked for farms contracted
to a number of Arkansas chicken companies.

1111
01:00:18,301 --> 01:00:23,501
[man in Spanish] We are a group
of six people who catch chickens.

1112
01:00:25,901 --> 01:00:29,501
In one day we lift 40,000 chickens.

1113
01:00:31,261 --> 01:00:34,061
We are catching two chickens in each hand.

1114
01:00:36,501 --> 01:00:42,301
My fingers are not in good shape anymore.

1115
01:00:42,381 --> 01:00:45,221
I cannot close them anymore.

1116
01:00:46,501 --> 01:00:48,501
Sometimes also when we sleep,

1117
01:00:48,581 --> 01:00:52,581
our hands twitch
like we are still catching chickens.

1118
01:00:54,061 --> 01:00:56,381
[Driver in English]
These environments are full of chemicals,

1119
01:00:56,461 --> 01:00:58,941
and these workers
often have no protective gear,

1120
01:00:59,021 --> 01:01:03,301
and chickens are vicious,
so it's a really hard job.

1121
01:01:07,901 --> 01:01:14,381
I've been told that some of the people
catching chickens are also children.

1122
01:01:17,621 --> 01:01:21,901
[man in Spanish]
Yes. Many kids. 15, 16, 17.

1123
01:01:21,981 --> 01:01:24,541
The child that is working in my group,

1124
01:01:25,061 --> 01:01:28,661
he had a fever, cough,

1125
01:01:28,741 --> 01:01:33,141
red swollen eyes because of the chemicals.

1126
01:01:33,221 --> 01:01:36,861
He cried and he nearly passed out.

1127
01:01:39,901 --> 01:01:41,781
[Mo in English] After meeting Carlos,

1128
01:01:41,861 --> 01:01:45,341
I learned that in 2023,
the US Department of Labor

1129
01:01:45,421 --> 01:01:50,181
{\an8}discovered over 100 children employed
by a contractor

1130
01:01:50,261 --> 01:01:54,541
{\an8}working illegally inside
nine major meat packing plants,

1131
01:01:54,621 --> 01:01:58,261
{\an8}including George's and Tyson Foods.

1132
01:02:09,901 --> 01:02:11,701
[Mo] That was someone the same age as me,

1133
01:02:12,301 --> 01:02:17,981
and I could see the physical toll
it had taken on his body.

1134
01:02:18,941 --> 01:02:21,021
It is... It's modern-day slavery.

1135
01:02:22,461 --> 01:02:23,741
It's the only way you can put it.

1136
01:02:27,021 --> 01:02:32,341
[Lymbery] The industry has managed
to put a veil over chicken,

1137
01:02:32,421 --> 01:02:37,661
to turn it into this...
what sounds like a fantastic commodity.

1138
01:02:37,741 --> 01:02:40,101
Almost too good to be true.

1139
01:02:40,181 --> 01:02:42,221
And that's because it is!

1140
01:02:42,901 --> 01:02:44,981
I knew a lot went into this business,

1141
01:02:45,061 --> 01:02:49,061
but when you actually meet people
that are behind the scenes...

1142
01:02:50,141 --> 01:02:51,621
[sighs heavily]

1143
01:02:51,701 --> 01:02:54,021
It really does change your outlook

1144
01:02:54,821 --> 01:02:57,141
on the food that... you...

1145
01:02:58,541 --> 01:02:59,461
love.

1146
01:03:00,221 --> 01:03:01,701
This is big, man.

1147
01:03:03,421 --> 01:03:06,141
And I understand,

1148
01:03:06,221 --> 01:03:09,181
as much as I get to go
down this rabbit hole,

1149
01:03:09,261 --> 01:03:11,581
there is a part of me that feels like,

1150
01:03:12,421 --> 01:03:15,501
"Am I gonna get men in black suits
that are gonna be, like,

1151
01:03:15,581 --> 01:03:17,421
outside of my house now?"

1152
01:03:24,621 --> 01:03:28,261
I can't believe how shady parts
of the Big Chicken industry are.

1153
01:03:30,861 --> 01:03:35,221
I've been told to go to Delaware,
where people are fighting back.

1154
01:03:36,141 --> 01:03:40,341
"Welcome to Delaware.
Endless discoveries."

1155
01:03:40,421 --> 01:03:43,021
It's like they built that sign
knowing I was coming, boy,

1156
01:03:43,101 --> 01:03:46,061
'cause that's what I'm doing, man.
I'm discovering, man.

1157
01:03:46,781 --> 01:03:51,021
Oh, man. I'm going mad,
guys, I'm going mad.

1158
01:03:51,101 --> 01:03:54,181
I'm eating this chicken and I'm going mad.

1159
01:03:56,341 --> 01:03:59,741
The chicken industry
is big business in Delaware,

1160
01:03:59,821 --> 01:04:02,861
and its effects are even being
reported on the radio.

1161
01:04:03,541 --> 01:04:07,661
[reporter] <i>...state's chicken industry
produces literally tons of chicken waste.</i>

1162
01:04:07,741 --> 01:04:09,981
<i>People who live near those chicken farms</i>

1163
01:04:10,061 --> 01:04:13,381
<i>are worried about how all those chickens
are affecting their health.</i>

1164
01:04:13,461 --> 01:04:15,301
[Mo] Here's what I learned
from the air waves.

1165
01:04:15,381 --> 01:04:17,501
Delaware has a ton of chickens,

1166
01:04:17,581 --> 01:04:19,901
and they produce a shitload of shit.

1167
01:04:19,981 --> 01:04:21,141
Sorry, Mum.

1168
01:04:21,221 --> 01:04:23,661
Now imagine all the chickens in America.

1169
01:04:23,741 --> 01:04:28,501
A year's worth of their chicken poop
could cover the whole of London.

1170
01:04:28,581 --> 01:04:30,701
{\an8}And Edinburgh. That's a lot of poo.

1171
01:04:34,221 --> 01:04:35,821
{\an8}- Yes, bro.
- Mo, how are ya?

1172
01:04:35,901 --> 01:04:37,661
{\an8}- I'm good. How are you, man?
- Nice to meet ya.

1173
01:04:37,741 --> 01:04:38,901
{\an8}[Mo] Whoo!

1174
01:04:38,981 --> 01:04:40,021
I brought the rain with me.

1175
01:04:40,101 --> 01:04:41,061
- Michael.
- You all right?

1176
01:04:41,741 --> 01:04:45,021
[Mo] Michael lives here,
and he's fighting back.

1177
01:04:45,101 --> 01:04:47,101
I've asked him to show me the issue.

1178
01:04:48,061 --> 01:04:50,181
When you see
some of these poultry barns out there...

1179
01:04:50,261 --> 01:04:52,381
- Okay.
- ...you see the fans on the very ends.

1180
01:04:52,461 --> 01:04:53,301
[Mo] Yeah, yeah.

1181
01:04:53,381 --> 01:04:55,101
Those fans aren't there to keep them cool.

1182
01:04:55,181 --> 01:04:58,341
They're there because these birds,
they're getting raised on a pile of waste.

1183
01:04:58,421 --> 01:05:00,501
- Yeah.
- In one of those barns, the newer ones,

1184
01:05:00,581 --> 01:05:02,141
you have 50,000 birds in a barn.

1185
01:05:02,221 --> 01:05:03,941
There's 50,000 birds' worth of waste,

1186
01:05:04,021 --> 01:05:06,581
and that doesn't get cleaned out
every flock, so they're on waste,

1187
01:05:06,661 --> 01:05:08,261
but there's ammonia in there.

1188
01:05:08,341 --> 01:05:10,501
- Oh, shit. Yeah.
- So that ammonia,

1189
01:05:10,581 --> 01:05:13,101
right, like, the chickens,
some of them go blind.

1190
01:05:13,181 --> 01:05:15,621
So they need to blow that air out

1191
01:05:15,701 --> 01:05:18,101
because if they don't,
it'll kill the chickens.

1192
01:05:18,181 --> 01:05:19,181
Mm. Okay.

1193
01:05:19,261 --> 01:05:21,861
If you're getting that air
out into the community,

1194
01:05:21,941 --> 01:05:23,861
what do you think's happening
to the community?

1195
01:05:23,941 --> 01:05:27,301
You're looking at a toxic stew of gases

1196
01:05:27,381 --> 01:05:29,861
that people are breathing in.
That's impactful to their health.

1197
01:05:29,941 --> 01:05:33,101
You see this?
You see where these neighbors live?

1198
01:05:33,181 --> 01:05:35,421
Fucking hell! That is so close!

1199
01:05:35,501 --> 01:05:38,141
These are veterans.
There's a veteran that lives here.

1200
01:05:38,221 --> 01:05:40,461
When he was fighting in Iraq,
what did they do?

1201
01:05:40,541 --> 01:05:44,181
Put up a bunch of chicken barns across
from his house blowing nasty air on him.

1202
01:05:44,261 --> 01:05:45,821
Oh, that stinks, man!

1203
01:05:46,581 --> 01:05:48,821
- That's what you would smell every day?
- Every day.

1204
01:05:48,901 --> 01:05:51,821
- You can smell it in the car!
- It gets stuck on your clothes!

1205
01:05:51,901 --> 01:05:56,061
That's wrong, 'cause these are beautiful
communities built on agriculture.

1206
01:05:56,141 --> 01:05:58,861
That's not to say
that agriculture is bad, it's not.

1207
01:05:58,941 --> 01:06:02,701
But there's a way to farm
that doesn't destroy the community,

1208
01:06:02,781 --> 01:06:04,941
destroy your neighbors' quality of life.

1209
01:06:05,861 --> 01:06:07,941
[Mo] I didn't think it would
get to a point where

1210
01:06:08,701 --> 01:06:12,021
there are people
that are massively affected

1211
01:06:12,101 --> 01:06:15,861
due to the quality of their air
and the water that they drink.

1212
01:06:17,661 --> 01:06:19,861
Now, this isn't just about the smell.

1213
01:06:19,941 --> 01:06:22,101
That huge plant right there

1214
01:06:22,181 --> 01:06:26,021
is one of the biggest
chicken waste facilities in Delaware.

1215
01:06:26,701 --> 01:06:29,141
These guys are cutting up
millions of birds here.

1216
01:06:29,221 --> 01:06:30,741
When you cut 'em up,
you gotta wash 'em off.

1217
01:06:30,821 --> 01:06:33,421
You got all the guts
and all that nasty stuff.

1218
01:06:33,501 --> 01:06:36,141
I'm probably doing it a little
less scientific than it really is,

1219
01:06:36,221 --> 01:06:39,181
but essentially,
they're taking that waste water

1220
01:06:39,261 --> 01:06:41,101
and spraying it on these fields.

1221
01:06:41,181 --> 01:06:43,781
But remember that
everybody's on well water, man.

1222
01:06:44,301 --> 01:06:46,941
Are you serious? Hey, that's fucked, man!

1223
01:06:47,461 --> 01:06:51,021
People can't breathe their air, can't go
drink their water, can't go outside.

1224
01:06:51,101 --> 01:06:52,781
Kids are getting sick, getting asthma,

1225
01:06:52,861 --> 01:06:55,141
so they could sell
cheap chicken in the store

1226
01:06:55,221 --> 01:06:57,941
for 99 cents a pound or $1.99 a pound.

1227
01:06:58,021 --> 01:06:59,421
Are you kidding me?

1228
01:06:59,501 --> 01:07:03,061
There's big problems, in an industry
that's way too powerful for its own good.

1229
01:07:05,061 --> 01:07:09,341
[Mo] Mountaire, the company accused of
polluting the air and water here,

1230
01:07:09,421 --> 01:07:14,061
settled a class action lawsuit
for $205 million,

1231
01:07:14,981 --> 01:07:16,861
but denied all liability.

1232
01:07:21,061 --> 01:07:23,821
I've been questioning everything.
I'm washing,

1233
01:07:23,901 --> 01:07:26,301
brushing my teeth
after what I've heard with water.

1234
01:07:28,141 --> 01:07:29,581
That's how paranoid I am.

1235
01:07:31,621 --> 01:07:35,061
When I started this,
I didn't know Big Chicken was a thing.

1236
01:07:36,381 --> 01:07:37,461
I didn't know.

1237
01:07:38,621 --> 01:07:41,021
I didn't know this was a thing, you know.

1238
01:07:41,901 --> 01:07:45,541
And I feel disappointed in myself
that I didn't know about it.

1239
01:07:46,181 --> 01:07:50,581
Then you get there. You're looking at
this plant right next to someone's house.

1240
01:07:52,021 --> 01:07:53,901
Really, really sad, man.

1241
01:07:55,901 --> 01:07:58,181
I just wanna stop eating it now.

1242
01:08:00,541 --> 01:08:04,701
I really do. I wanna just stop eating it.
I don't wanna do this no more.

1243
01:08:06,501 --> 01:08:07,581
Yeah.

1244
01:08:09,141 --> 01:08:11,141
[intriguing music playing]

1245
01:08:17,061 --> 01:08:18,581
There is something quite nice

1246
01:08:18,661 --> 01:08:22,261
in having your family around
and having the bargain bucket.

1247
01:08:23,661 --> 01:08:25,541
But at, like, what cost?

1248
01:08:26,181 --> 01:08:27,941
You start to look at the balance.

1249
01:08:28,701 --> 01:08:30,821
I didn't ever think of that
before I'd done this.

1250
01:08:30,901 --> 01:08:33,101
I was like,
"It's agriculture. We all gotta survive."

1251
01:08:34,101 --> 01:08:36,541
But do we need to survive at that extent?

1252
01:08:37,861 --> 01:08:40,901
I would have been guilty of saying,
"It's just chicken though, man."

1253
01:08:40,981 --> 01:08:42,301
"Everyone chill out."

1254
01:08:42,381 --> 01:08:45,901
But it's until you hear the stories
of the people that are really affected,

1255
01:08:45,981 --> 01:08:49,741
it's until you eat the food
and someone tells you you've got

1256
01:08:49,821 --> 01:08:52,741
an addiction to chicken
which is similar to cocaine.

1257
01:08:52,821 --> 01:08:55,341
And you're like, "What?"

1258
01:08:55,941 --> 01:08:59,621
And I think sometimes,
if there's that much money to be made,

1259
01:08:59,701 --> 01:09:04,101
then there's also a lot of secrets
to be hidden at the same time.

1260
01:09:04,181 --> 01:09:06,661
And I'm seeing that here, in America,

1261
01:09:07,661 --> 01:09:09,101
but back home...

1262
01:09:10,581 --> 01:09:12,301
How deep does it really go?

1263
01:09:15,421 --> 01:09:19,981
[Lymbery] The mega farming of animals
is environmentally hugely damaging.

1264
01:09:20,061 --> 01:09:24,581
The chickens are raised on grain,
including soya,

1265
01:09:24,661 --> 01:09:29,421
that comes from rainforest areas,
causing deforestation.

1266
01:09:29,501 --> 01:09:33,701
And in the UK,
artificial fertilizers, pesticides,

1267
01:09:33,781 --> 01:09:37,341
is causing widespread decline in our soil.

1268
01:09:37,421 --> 01:09:40,181
Our soil is literally disappearing.

1269
01:09:41,621 --> 01:09:46,781
Scientists tell us we have just decades
left before our soil is gone.

1270
01:09:46,861 --> 01:09:48,221
No soil, no food.

1271
01:09:48,821 --> 01:09:49,901
Game over.

1272
01:09:52,261 --> 01:09:53,741
[Mo] Ready to go.

1273
01:09:54,621 --> 01:09:56,461
Let's get the hell out of here, man.

1274
01:09:57,461 --> 01:09:59,461
[energetic hip-hop song playing]

1275
01:10:08,261 --> 01:10:10,861
Got the KFC drive thru, you know.

1276
01:10:10,941 --> 01:10:16,261
They do this smart thing now,
you order and it goes, "Anything else?"

1277
01:10:16,341 --> 01:10:17,741
- Hello.
- [attendant] <i>Hi, sir.</i>

1278
01:10:17,821 --> 01:10:19,501
Can I get a Mini Variety Meal?

1279
01:10:19,581 --> 01:10:21,021
[attendant] <i>Anything else, sir?</i>

1280
01:10:21,541 --> 01:10:24,941
Can I also get a portion of wings?

1281
01:10:25,021 --> 01:10:26,261
<i>Anything else?</i>

1282
01:10:26,341 --> 01:10:28,941
- Thank you. See you.
- Take care. See you later, yeah?

1283
01:10:29,021 --> 01:10:32,981
You see?
That "Anything else? Anything else?"

1284
01:10:33,061 --> 01:10:34,741
I didn't want all that stuff.

1285
01:10:37,421 --> 01:10:40,421
This food is so problematic.

1286
01:10:40,501 --> 01:10:41,341
[chuckles]

1287
01:10:43,181 --> 01:10:47,101
The journey that I went on
opened my eyes up to a lot.

1288
01:10:48,181 --> 01:10:51,141
People, I can't ignore
what I've learnt on this trip.

1289
01:10:51,221 --> 01:10:53,701
Since I've been back,
I've been busy researching.

1290
01:10:53,781 --> 01:10:56,541
It looks like KFC are on a rampage.

1291
01:10:58,421 --> 01:11:02,901
[reporter] <i>KFC has announced plans
to open 500 new restaurants</i>

1292
01:11:02,981 --> 01:11:05,461
<i>in the UK and Ireland
within the next decade.</i>

1293
01:11:05,541 --> 01:11:09,101
Mo said that he doesn't wanna be
an investigative journalist,

1294
01:11:09,181 --> 01:11:11,901
which I understand,
but I think at some point,

1295
01:11:11,981 --> 01:11:14,661
if he wants to really delve
deeper into this industry,

1296
01:11:14,741 --> 01:11:18,381
he's gonna have to go
and put his journalist hat on and...

1297
01:11:18,461 --> 01:11:19,541
dig a little deeper.

1298
01:11:20,061 --> 01:11:23,581
[automated operator]
<i>Hello, and welcome to KFC Head Office.</i>

1299
01:11:23,661 --> 01:11:27,701
<i>If you have a customer-care related query,
please press one.</i>

1300
01:11:27,781 --> 01:11:28,621
[phone beeps]

1301
01:11:31,501 --> 01:11:36,541
<i>All customer-care queries must be
submitted in writing using the web form...</i>

1302
01:11:38,461 --> 01:11:41,661
[Mo] It's not just about
how this experiment has affected me,

1303
01:11:41,741 --> 01:11:45,981
but, as market leaders, I just wanna have
a conversation about how their food

1304
01:11:46,061 --> 01:11:49,981
has impacted our health
and the environment.

1305
01:11:50,501 --> 01:11:52,981
This is my email from KFC.

1306
01:11:53,061 --> 01:11:56,581
"Thank you very much for reaching out
and sharing more about your documentary."

1307
01:11:56,661 --> 01:11:58,701
"It sounds like
a really interesting project."

1308
01:11:58,781 --> 01:12:02,781
"While we won't be able to contribute
directly, we wish you every success

1309
01:12:02,861 --> 01:12:05,021
as you bring this story to life."

1310
01:12:05,101 --> 01:12:09,501
"Thank you again for getting in touch
and best of luck with the film."

1311
01:12:09,581 --> 01:12:13,821
They've basically just treated
me like a dickhead.com.

1312
01:12:17,021 --> 01:12:19,221
I did get one breakthrough, though.

1313
01:12:19,301 --> 01:12:24,381
The big bossman CEO from the British
Poultry Council is willing to speak to me.

1314
01:12:26,301 --> 01:12:29,461
We asked to meet him at one of
the factory farms they represent,

1315
01:12:29,541 --> 01:12:32,661
but he said, "Access will be difficult."

1316
01:12:32,741 --> 01:12:34,301
Hmm, interesting.

1317
01:12:34,381 --> 01:12:37,741
So we settled for this cute
little city farm instead.

1318
01:12:39,141 --> 01:12:42,181
- What's up, Rich? How you doing, man?
- Yeah, good, thanks. You?

1319
01:12:42,261 --> 01:12:44,621
Got you out here in the middle
of Hackney, bruv. You okay?

1320
01:12:44,701 --> 01:12:45,901
Yeah, very well.

1321
01:12:45,981 --> 01:12:48,341
Looking like an undercover cop
right now, man.

1322
01:12:49,661 --> 01:12:54,781
{\an8}We, as the UK, we slaughter
around 20 million birds a week.

1323
01:12:54,861 --> 01:12:56,581
- A week?
- A week.

1324
01:12:56,661 --> 01:12:59,181
But think about it
in terms of numbers of people.

1325
01:12:59,261 --> 01:13:01,341
65 million people.
Not everybody eats meat.

1326
01:13:01,421 --> 01:13:04,061
- Say 60 million people eat chicken.
- Yeah.

1327
01:13:04,141 --> 01:13:05,981
Half a chicken per person per week.

1328
01:13:06,061 --> 01:13:07,461
That's not a lot.

1329
01:13:07,541 --> 01:13:10,021
And there's hunger in this country.

1330
01:13:10,101 --> 01:13:13,341
Fundamentally, until we get everybody fed,

1331
01:13:13,421 --> 01:13:15,501
nothing else really matters.

1332
01:13:15,581 --> 01:13:18,981
And yes, we have to be very cognizant of

1333
01:13:19,061 --> 01:13:22,421
how we treat people,
the environment, the birds,

1334
01:13:22,501 --> 01:13:25,181
but we can manage that,
and if we do it right,

1335
01:13:25,261 --> 01:13:28,261
we've got a food source
for the world, not just the UK.

1336
01:13:28,341 --> 01:13:32,621
Very politician-y, Rich, man.
You might start kissing some babies, bruv.

1337
01:13:32,701 --> 01:13:33,901
Yeah, but that's...

1338
01:13:36,421 --> 01:13:38,381
When I went on this journey,

1339
01:13:38,461 --> 01:13:43,141
I got to see every aspect
of the chicken industry.

1340
01:13:43,221 --> 01:13:46,141
What I wasn't prepared for was the waste.

1341
01:13:46,221 --> 01:13:48,741
What are we doing here
when it comes to waste?

1342
01:13:48,821 --> 01:13:51,941
There are accusations and criticisms
about our industry,

1343
01:13:52,021 --> 01:13:54,421
and what we do with the waste,

1344
01:13:54,501 --> 01:13:58,461
but I can say for sure
that all of the waste,

1345
01:13:58,541 --> 01:14:02,061
and we're talking about farms here,
rather than processing plants,

1346
01:14:02,141 --> 01:14:03,341
is very much controlled.

1347
01:14:03,421 --> 01:14:05,901
And what about the waste
from the processing plant?

1348
01:14:05,981 --> 01:14:08,901
That, similarly, is very much controlled

1349
01:14:08,981 --> 01:14:11,781
so that it's not a risk, and not harmful.

1350
01:14:11,861 --> 01:14:13,501
So there's no corners cut in the UK?

1351
01:14:13,581 --> 01:14:15,621
There's not. There's no benefit.

1352
01:14:15,701 --> 01:14:17,701
But you got your PR lady here, man.

1353
01:14:17,781 --> 01:14:21,381
She's gonna jump in any minute, man.
"Hey, can we just stop that?"

1354
01:14:21,461 --> 01:14:22,501
[Mo laughing]

1355
01:14:23,701 --> 01:14:26,381
- [Griffiths] We try and be good neighbors...
- [Mo] Yeah.

1356
01:14:26,461 --> 01:14:28,541
[Griffiths] ...and not impact on the people.

1357
01:14:31,261 --> 01:14:33,701
[Mo] After our chat,
it came out in the news

1358
01:14:33,781 --> 01:14:35,701
that chicken producers Avara Foods,

1359
01:14:35,781 --> 01:14:37,301
who Richard represents,

1360
01:14:37,381 --> 01:14:39,901
is currently facing the largest ever

1361
01:14:39,981 --> 01:14:43,301
environmental legal claim
in British history

1362
01:14:43,381 --> 01:14:46,461
for allegedly polluting
three of our rivers.

1363
01:14:47,021 --> 01:14:49,101
[narrator]
<i>There's over 20 million chickens</i>

1364
01:14:49,181 --> 01:14:50,661
<i>in the River Wye catchment area,</i>

1365
01:14:50,741 --> 01:14:53,821
<i>and they create more manure
than the land can absorb.</i>

1366
01:14:53,901 --> 01:14:57,661
{\an8}The industrial chicken farmers you buy
your supplies from for your restaurants

1367
01:14:57,741 --> 01:15:00,941
has literally polluted
the River Wye to death.

1368
01:15:03,061 --> 01:15:05,061
[ducks honking]

1369
01:15:06,501 --> 01:15:08,781
- What's going on, bruv?
- What you telling me, bruv?

1370
01:15:08,861 --> 01:15:10,261
Cluck cluck, rude boy!

1371
01:15:10,341 --> 01:15:11,941
- Come in, bruv.
- Wagwan?

1372
01:15:12,021 --> 01:15:13,941
- Yeah, G. All blessed.
- Come in.

1373
01:15:14,021 --> 01:15:17,221
[David] You went and saw
that Richard brother, yeah? How was that?

1374
01:15:17,301 --> 01:15:20,861
I respect that he came
and had a conversation with me,

1375
01:15:20,941 --> 01:15:24,021
but at the same time,
feel like I was just talking to an AI.

1376
01:15:24,101 --> 01:15:27,421
So I'm asking him questions
and it's like, "Well, you know,

1377
01:15:27,501 --> 01:15:29,021
there's a lot of people to feed."

1378
01:15:29,101 --> 01:15:31,941
"Could we do more? Possibly."

1379
01:15:32,021 --> 01:15:35,981
So where does that leave you now?
Like, what's next?

1380
01:15:36,061 --> 01:15:39,741
It's a tricky one,
because nobody wants to talk to me, innit?

1381
01:15:39,821 --> 01:15:42,261
I got an email back from KFC.

1382
01:15:43,101 --> 01:15:44,301
KFC Global.

1383
01:15:44,381 --> 01:15:45,901
- Okay, big time.
- Yeah.

1384
01:15:45,981 --> 01:15:48,381
- I know, right?
- Let me see that.

1385
01:15:51,021 --> 01:15:54,101
There's a number here, you know.
You should try calling them.

1386
01:15:54,181 --> 01:15:55,581
- Let's call them.
- Yeah?

1387
01:15:55,661 --> 01:15:57,781
[line ringing]

1388
01:15:57,861 --> 01:15:59,381
[tense music playing]

1389
01:16:00,341 --> 01:16:01,981
[click on other end of line]

1390
01:16:02,061 --> 01:16:03,061
Hello?

1391
01:16:06,421 --> 01:16:07,501
Hello?

1392
01:16:09,781 --> 01:16:11,141
It's me, Mo Gilligan.

1393
01:16:11,221 --> 01:16:13,101
Uh, I sent you an email

1394
01:16:14,221 --> 01:16:16,741
about a documentary about fried chicken.

1395
01:16:17,941 --> 01:16:18,981
Hello?

1396
01:16:20,141 --> 01:16:21,221
Hello?

1397
01:16:25,861 --> 01:16:26,861
They put me on mute.

1398
01:16:26,941 --> 01:16:28,981
- 'Cause they picked up.
- Is it?

1399
01:16:30,461 --> 01:16:31,501
[Mo] Hello?

1400
01:16:33,101 --> 01:16:38,261
I am recording the call to potentially use
in a documentary. Is it okay to continue?

1401
01:16:38,341 --> 01:16:39,581
[disconnect tone drones]

1402
01:16:40,781 --> 01:16:43,221
Okay.

1403
01:16:44,181 --> 01:16:45,141
Rah.

1404
01:16:46,181 --> 01:16:47,741
- She hung up.
- Yeah.

1405
01:16:47,821 --> 01:16:50,061
Oh, my days. Do you know what?

1406
01:16:50,141 --> 01:16:52,861
Do you know what she done?
She answered the phone and put it on mute.

1407
01:16:52,941 --> 01:16:55,141
- Yeah, and was listening.
- Just listening.

1408
01:16:55,221 --> 01:16:56,461
That's wild, you know.

1409
01:16:56,541 --> 01:16:59,261
That's the closest I've got
from anyone from KFC.

1410
01:16:59,341 --> 01:17:00,741
- Yeah.
- Like, the closest.

1411
01:17:00,821 --> 01:17:02,341
You left your number there.

1412
01:17:02,421 --> 01:17:05,701
Why'd you leave your number
and not pick up? What's the point?

1413
01:17:07,221 --> 01:17:09,621
I guess it's not even
a big surprise to learn that,

1414
01:17:09,701 --> 01:17:11,901
like many leading fast food brands,

1415
01:17:11,981 --> 01:17:15,861
KFC works with online influencers
with millions of followers

1416
01:17:15,941 --> 01:17:20,021
to promote its products
and brand to younger audiences.

1417
01:17:21,741 --> 01:17:24,021
{\an8}<i>Hello, my friends.
I'm Niko, and I'm on a farm.</i>

1418
01:17:24,101 --> 01:17:28,421
Ah, Niko. I like Niko, man.
He's good peoples, man.

1419
01:17:28,501 --> 01:17:30,581
{\an8}<i>Let's go behind the bucket.</i>

1420
01:17:31,621 --> 01:17:32,661
<i>Oh my God.</i>

1421
01:17:33,821 --> 01:17:37,261
{\an8}<i>Look at all those chickens!
There's no cages here.</i>

1422
01:17:37,341 --> 01:17:40,701
{\an8}<i>I thought there was, like,
4,000 chickens in one small cage.</i>

1423
01:17:40,781 --> 01:17:42,781
{\an8}<i>No, no. There is no cages.</i>

1424
01:17:42,861 --> 01:17:45,421
{\an8}<i>We're very proud
of our animal welfare here,</i>

1425
01:17:45,501 --> 01:17:47,861
So you're showing the healthy side

1426
01:17:47,941 --> 01:17:51,621
{\an8}by using someone
who's got a really big platform,

1427
01:17:52,541 --> 01:17:56,501
but you're controlling
what they're seeing behind the curtain.

1428
01:17:57,141 --> 01:18:01,981
{\an8}KFC chose to bring Niko down here
when the birds were really small and cute.

1429
01:18:02,061 --> 01:18:07,021
{\an8}They were maybe 15 to 20 days old.
We've come back when they're 33 days old.

1430
01:18:07,101 --> 01:18:09,621
{\an8}- You really do care about these chickens?
- Yeah, we do.

1431
01:18:09,701 --> 01:18:10,741
{\an8}What about these chickens?

1432
01:18:10,821 --> 01:18:13,621
{\an8}Do you care about birds
like these three here

1433
01:18:13,701 --> 01:18:16,901
{\an8}who have got to the point
where they can't stand up anymore?

1434
01:18:16,981 --> 01:18:19,181
{\an8}They just can't handle the weight.

1435
01:18:19,261 --> 01:18:21,061
{\an8}[Mo] I feel like it's very sinister,

1436
01:18:21,141 --> 01:18:25,181
'cause it's like a new digital age
of how you can manipulate the market.

1437
01:18:25,781 --> 01:18:28,421
{\an8}[Tony] <i>...the birds plenty of room to roam...</i>

1438
01:18:28,501 --> 01:18:30,621
{\an8}[Mo] It's almost like greenwashing.

1439
01:18:31,381 --> 01:18:35,221
{\an8}I can probably find out more information
about your secret recipe

1440
01:18:35,301 --> 01:18:42,061
than I can about where you get your
chickens from and how they are treated.

1441
01:18:42,141 --> 01:18:44,701
{\an8}<i>Thanks for having me.
I'm loving the farm, it looks incredible.</i>

1442
01:18:44,781 --> 01:18:47,461
The question is, what are you hiding?

1443
01:18:47,541 --> 01:18:49,541
[suspenseful music playing]

1444
01:18:50,301 --> 01:18:54,741
[Mo] I found the man whose footage told
a very different story to KFC's own video.

1445
01:18:56,021 --> 01:18:58,341
He sent me some coordinates,

1446
01:18:58,421 --> 01:19:01,101
'cause him and his team
are going back in tonight.

1447
01:19:01,861 --> 01:19:06,781
I didn't think chicken would have me
in Lincolnshire at nighttime

1448
01:19:06,861 --> 01:19:08,501
following coordinates.

1449
01:19:08,581 --> 01:19:11,661
Feels very... It feels a bit mad,
I'm not gonna lie to you people.

1450
01:19:12,541 --> 01:19:14,981
I feel like a real journalist right now.

1451
01:19:15,061 --> 01:19:17,101
I feel like Martin Kemp.

1452
01:19:17,181 --> 01:19:18,941
That's his name, innit? Martin Kemp.

1453
01:19:19,021 --> 01:19:22,541
Oh, no, that's the fucking guy
from Spandau Ballet. Ross Kemp.

1454
01:19:22,621 --> 01:19:23,661
[laughs]

1455
01:19:24,701 --> 01:19:29,181
Here in Lincolnshire, they slaughter
the most chickens in the UK.

1456
01:19:30,061 --> 01:19:33,781
Like everything, if you follow the money,
you'll find the truth.

1457
01:19:33,861 --> 01:19:39,181
And get this, the second-largest
chicken producers in the UK, Moy Park,

1458
01:19:39,261 --> 01:19:42,821
supply 25% of the chicken we eat,

1459
01:19:42,901 --> 01:19:46,821
including what ends up
in some of KFC's buckets.

1460
01:19:47,461 --> 01:19:48,621
But wait for it...

1461
01:19:50,141 --> 01:19:52,541
This UK company

1462
01:19:52,621 --> 01:19:58,181
is actually owned by the second-biggest
American chicken producers after Tyson,

1463
01:19:58,261 --> 01:19:59,381
Pilgrim's,

1464
01:20:00,261 --> 01:20:05,061
and they are owned
by the kings of chicken, JBS,

1465
01:20:05,141 --> 01:20:10,021
who produce up to
5 billion chickens a year globally.

1466
01:20:11,501 --> 01:20:13,541
I think that's him there, you know.

1467
01:20:14,541 --> 01:20:15,821
- Matthew!
- Hiya.

1468
01:20:15,901 --> 01:20:18,461
- What's up, my guy? You okay?
- Hey, Mo, nice to meet you.

1469
01:20:18,541 --> 01:20:20,581
What's good, man? Come on, hop in, man.

1470
01:20:22,541 --> 01:20:25,501
The KFC farm's just on the left over here,

1471
01:20:25,581 --> 01:20:27,781
and we're gonna go back in tonight.

1472
01:20:27,861 --> 01:20:29,821
You know, we filmed it three years ago,

1473
01:20:29,901 --> 01:20:32,901
and the video got 12 million views
or something like that.

1474
01:20:32,981 --> 01:20:36,381
So it'd be really interesting to see,
has anything changed?

1475
01:20:36,461 --> 01:20:39,021
Um, I'm gonna meet my two colleagues.

1476
01:20:39,981 --> 01:20:44,101
One's a professional investigator,
highly experienced.

1477
01:20:44,181 --> 01:20:47,181
I think we can film them from behind.

1478
01:20:47,261 --> 01:20:48,781
[suspenseful music playing]

1479
01:20:50,341 --> 01:20:51,381
Hey, what's up, guys?

1480
01:20:51,461 --> 01:20:52,861
[masked man 1] Hey. Hiya.

1481
01:20:52,941 --> 01:20:54,861
- Wow, this is real stuff, boy.
- [Glover] Yeah.

1482
01:20:54,941 --> 01:20:57,981
This is like <i>Call of Duty.</i>
I'm a bit scared, man. This is real.

1483
01:20:58,061 --> 01:21:01,541
- [masked man 1] What you scared of?
- I don't know, I've never seen...

1484
01:21:01,621 --> 01:21:04,821
- [masked man 1] You're scared of us?
- Because yous wearing balaclavas, man.

1485
01:21:05,701 --> 01:21:08,941
[masked man 1] So it is Moy Park farm.

1486
01:21:09,021 --> 01:21:12,981
We'll be coming in from the north.

1487
01:21:13,061 --> 01:21:15,421
There's two houses.
There's people living there.

1488
01:21:15,501 --> 01:21:17,861
[Mo] What risk does that kind of pose?

1489
01:21:17,941 --> 01:21:20,941
[masked man 1] The biggest risk
would be a farmer or a worker

1490
01:21:21,021 --> 01:21:23,541
deciding to take things
into their own hands if they found you.

1491
01:21:23,621 --> 01:21:25,901
I know some of these farmers
have got shotguns.

1492
01:21:25,981 --> 01:21:29,381
I've had a drone shot at before,
so, you know, it happens.

1493
01:21:29,461 --> 01:21:31,101
- Really?
- [masked man 1] Yeah.

1494
01:21:31,181 --> 01:21:32,221
Oh, man.

1495
01:21:33,461 --> 01:21:35,501
Me personally,
I don't give a shit anymore.

1496
01:21:35,581 --> 01:21:38,221
I just feel like we need to...
Like, somebody's gotta do it,

1497
01:21:38,301 --> 01:21:41,141
and so, therefore,
we've just gotta go and do it.

1498
01:21:41,221 --> 01:21:44,461
I'm gonna follow you. I'm gonna observe.
I'm not gonna go across.

1499
01:21:44,541 --> 01:21:46,101
Can we talk you into coming in?

1500
01:21:46,181 --> 01:21:47,541
- I wish... I would love to...
- No?

1501
01:21:47,621 --> 01:21:50,181
- ...but my girlfriend said no.
- Okay.

1502
01:21:54,181 --> 01:21:58,501
[Mo] It's early hours, and the team are
heading into one of Moy Park's farms.

1503
01:21:58,581 --> 01:22:00,181
I'm genuinely worried for them.

1504
01:22:01,941 --> 01:22:06,221
Maybe they might have different measures
if they have intruders here.

1505
01:22:06,301 --> 01:22:07,261
Know what I mean?

1506
01:22:09,221 --> 01:22:11,461
Yeah, man. They need to be careful, man.

1507
01:22:13,461 --> 01:22:15,221
I do feel a bit on edge, though.

1508
01:22:16,821 --> 01:22:18,821
I just feel like someone might turn up.

1509
01:22:19,661 --> 01:22:22,941
I don't think you realize,
I don't do stuff like this, you know?

1510
01:22:34,581 --> 01:22:37,901
<i>I'm just messaging
just to say we're here now,</i>

1511
01:22:38,541 --> 01:22:41,301
so I'll just show you what I can see.

1512
01:22:42,421 --> 01:22:45,021
We haven't managed to get access as yet.

1513
01:22:45,101 --> 01:22:47,661
<i>There's basically three separate locks,</i>

1514
01:22:47,741 --> 01:22:51,141
and then they've put
this tamper tag on here.

1515
01:22:51,221 --> 01:22:54,741
This is a lot more security
than when we came three years ago.

1516
01:22:54,821 --> 01:22:58,261
I'm sorry I'm speaking quietly.
We just don't wanna alert anybody.

1517
01:22:58,341 --> 01:23:00,261
<i>We have found a wheelie bin,</i>

1518
01:23:00,341 --> 01:23:03,421
but I just wanna show you what's inside.

1519
01:23:07,461 --> 01:23:10,421
Probably about two weeks old
are the birds there.

1520
01:23:10,501 --> 01:23:14,261
The smell, Mo, is just disgusting.
I'm having to hold my breath.

1521
01:23:16,781 --> 01:23:18,781
[suspenseful music continuing]

1522
01:23:23,501 --> 01:23:25,981
<i>We've found quite a lot
of the wheelie bins here,</i>

1523
01:23:26,061 --> 01:23:28,301
but interestingly,
they've locked these as well.

1524
01:23:28,381 --> 01:23:31,101
<i>They don't want people
filming the birds inside,</i>

1525
01:23:31,181 --> 01:23:34,421
but all of these ones here
are full and locked.

1526
01:23:35,461 --> 01:23:37,821
<i>So... we'll head back.</i>

1527
01:23:38,741 --> 01:23:39,661
See you soon.

1528
01:23:39,741 --> 01:23:44,901
I guess that the work that Matthew
and his team done before...

1529
01:23:46,461 --> 01:23:48,221
definitely ruffled some feathers.

1530
01:23:50,901 --> 01:23:52,301
How you feeling, man?

1531
01:23:52,381 --> 01:23:56,901
You know, it does feel to me like,
rather than, like, fix the problem

1532
01:23:56,981 --> 01:24:00,661
of these Frankenchickens
who can't stand up,

1533
01:24:00,741 --> 01:24:02,221
their bodies can't take it...

1534
01:24:02,301 --> 01:24:05,621
Rather than fix that problem,
they're just adding loads of security.

1535
01:24:05,701 --> 01:24:08,501
It's a picture that's happening
across the whole of the UK.

1536
01:24:08,581 --> 01:24:09,461
[Mo] Mmm.

1537
01:24:09,541 --> 01:24:11,181
So it's not just this farm.

1538
01:24:11,261 --> 01:24:12,821
There's many farms.

1539
01:24:12,901 --> 01:24:16,261
So it's just hidden from view, and that's...

1540
01:24:16,341 --> 01:24:17,861
To me, I feel that's very wrong.

1541
01:24:19,461 --> 01:24:21,941
[Mo] It's the same patterns, man.

1542
01:24:22,021 --> 01:24:26,821
It's not a coincidence we just went there
and there's just a few more locks, like...

1543
01:24:26,901 --> 01:24:31,181
Um, it's not a coincidence
that no one will get back to me.

1544
01:24:31,701 --> 01:24:34,101
It's just how powerful this thing is.

1545
01:24:35,181 --> 01:24:36,701
Not long after we met,

1546
01:24:36,781 --> 01:24:41,301
the investigators sent me footage of
an Avara farm they did manage to get into.

1547
01:24:41,381 --> 01:24:46,861
That's the same company accused of
polluting our rivers with chicken waste.

1548
01:24:46,941 --> 01:24:48,541
It looks horrible, man.

1549
01:24:49,381 --> 01:24:51,781
There's a human part of you that says,

1550
01:24:51,861 --> 01:24:55,741
no animal should ever
have to live and suffer like that.

1551
01:24:55,821 --> 01:24:58,221
That's the chicken
that I've probably eaten,

1552
01:24:58,301 --> 01:25:00,221
and you're probably eating too.

1553
01:25:01,501 --> 01:25:03,501
That is the stark reality,

1554
01:25:05,461 --> 01:25:09,461
that throughout this whole experiment,
this industry's been so secretive

1555
01:25:09,541 --> 01:25:11,421
and so silent,

1556
01:25:13,221 --> 01:25:15,781
but when you see
what's behind closed doors,

1557
01:25:16,741 --> 01:25:18,821
I understand why it's so silent now.

1558
01:25:25,261 --> 01:25:27,861
[Lymbery] Cheap chicken is far from cheap.

1559
01:25:27,941 --> 01:25:32,421
Globally, nearly three-quarters
of the world's antibiotics

1560
01:25:32,501 --> 01:25:34,341
are fed to farmed animals,

1561
01:25:34,421 --> 01:25:38,661
essentially to ward off
the diseases inherent in factory farms.

1562
01:25:38,741 --> 01:25:40,781
If we carry on as we are doing,

1563
01:25:40,861 --> 01:25:43,421
then the World Health Organization warns

1564
01:25:43,501 --> 01:25:47,941
that because of the rise
in antibiotic resistance,

1565
01:25:48,021 --> 01:25:52,581
then we could be facing
a post-antibiotic era,

1566
01:25:52,661 --> 01:25:56,901
where currently treatable diseases
could once again kill.

1567
01:26:03,221 --> 01:26:07,501
[Mo] Twenty-four hours
and the experiment will be done.

1568
01:26:07,581 --> 01:26:10,581
But having it for breakfast,
with a do-rag on,

1569
01:26:11,901 --> 01:26:15,221
I feel like the most Blackest man ever,
bruv. Know what I mean?

1570
01:26:18,461 --> 01:26:19,861
Eating this food now,

1571
01:26:21,901 --> 01:26:24,141
I'll be real with you,
I'm just bored of it, man.

1572
01:26:24,661 --> 01:26:27,341
I say that as I take another bite, though.

1573
01:26:29,461 --> 01:26:31,461
[energetic rap music playing]

1574
01:26:37,341 --> 01:26:43,341
[Mo] It's only right I spend my last meal
with the boys back in South London.

1575
01:26:43,421 --> 01:26:45,901
{\an8}What's going on? Rah, boy!

1576
01:26:45,981 --> 01:26:50,701
{\an8}I can't lie, I thought it'd be like,
"Surprise! It's the last drumstick!"

1577
01:26:53,981 --> 01:26:55,221
Wow. Yeah.

1578
01:26:55,301 --> 01:26:59,021
[server] OG wings,
and this is the new barbie fry.

1579
01:26:59,101 --> 01:27:01,341
[David] You don't want
none of that, right?

1580
01:27:01,421 --> 01:27:03,661
- [all laughing]
- Imagine the last piece.

1581
01:27:05,781 --> 01:27:09,101
[overlapping chatter, laughter]

1582
01:27:09,701 --> 01:27:13,981
- Congratulations, bro. Last meal.
- Nah, man. Yeah, no, I appreciate it, man.

1583
01:27:14,061 --> 01:27:16,781
Where is your mind at, like, right now?

1584
01:27:16,861 --> 01:27:19,981
[Mo] Yeah, I definitely feel like
I need to get back to myself,

1585
01:27:20,061 --> 01:27:23,901
and I was really, really shocked
about some of the stuff.

1586
01:27:23,981 --> 01:27:25,341
You can't unsee it.

1587
01:27:26,661 --> 01:27:31,061
And I didn't think the journey
would get that hard, and it did.

1588
01:27:31,141 --> 01:27:34,061
It was hard. To listen to the stories...

1589
01:27:34,141 --> 01:27:38,221
Yeah, it was just hard. You wanna stay
and you wanna fight the fight as well,

1590
01:27:38,301 --> 01:27:42,021
but it does give you
a real understanding of

1591
01:27:42,101 --> 01:27:46,541
the corners that are cut
to make X amount of money,

1592
01:27:46,621 --> 01:27:49,541
and it doesn't matter who's in their way.

1593
01:27:50,261 --> 01:27:53,181
And then it starts to
make you think of, like,

1594
01:27:53,261 --> 01:27:54,781
if this is happening now,

1595
01:27:55,621 --> 01:27:59,061
what's that going to look like
in five, 10, 15 years' time?

1596
01:28:00,021 --> 01:28:02,501
[friend 1] You definitely went down
real dark paths,

1597
01:28:02,581 --> 01:28:05,781
and, like, I'm also just proud
that you took this journey.

1598
01:28:05,861 --> 01:28:07,421
- [Mo] Thank you, man.
- So, yeah, man.

1599
01:28:11,461 --> 01:28:13,261
[Mo] People, I done it.

1600
01:28:13,341 --> 01:28:17,501
After an unbroken streak
of 84 fried chicken meals,

1601
01:28:17,581 --> 01:28:20,141
it's finally time to call it a day.

1602
01:28:23,701 --> 01:28:24,701
[groans]

1603
01:28:26,461 --> 01:28:29,701
First meal that is not fried chicken.

1604
01:28:30,261 --> 01:28:35,821
I got my coffee, I got my oat bar,
and I got my banana. Finally.

1605
01:28:36,901 --> 01:28:38,141
Finally!

1606
01:28:40,221 --> 01:28:41,221
[satisfied sigh]

1607
01:28:43,861 --> 01:28:45,461
I'm going back to visit Dr. Abood

1608
01:28:45,541 --> 01:28:49,381
to see what a month
of eating fried chicken has done to me.

1609
01:28:49,461 --> 01:28:51,581
- All right, here we go.
- Let's get you up.

1610
01:28:52,461 --> 01:28:55,141
[Abood] You have gained three kilograms,

1611
